%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
### Stat Counter
Here is an example component of a basic stat display:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.wlx

Stat[Text_, OptionsPattern[]] := With[{
      Count = OptionValue["Count"]
},

      <div class="text-center text-gray-600 m-4 p-4 rounded bg-gray-100 flex flex-col">
          <Count />
          <span class="text-md"><Text /></span>
      </div>
]

Options[Stat] = {"Count" -> 1};

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.slide

# Basic counter

<Stat Count={11}>Number of publications</Stat>

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: slide
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
<dummy >
# Basic counter

<div class="text-center text-gray-600 m-4 p-4 rounded bg-gray-100 flex flex-col">FrontEndExecutable[52ac37fe-5353-4c6b-8a61-a51c06901581]<span class="text-md">Number of publications</span>RVJSEvent["64203b44-12c6-4530-8995-aa27baa77b3e","Slide"]</div></dummy>

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
To make it count up from 0, we need to update the displayed number using a timer that starts when `SlideEventListener` fires the "Slide" event. To display the changing value, we use `HTMLView` (though `TextView` could also work):

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
To make it count up from 0, we need to update the displayed number using a timer that starts when `SlideEventListener` fires the "Slide" event. To display the changing value, we use `HTMLView` (though `TextView` could also work):

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Fade: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.wlx
Stat[Text_, OptionsPattern[]] := Module[{
      cnt = 0,
      task
}, With[{
      ev = CreateUUID[],
      HTMLCounter = HTMLView[cnt // Offload],
      max = OptionValue["Count"]
},

      EventHandler[ev, {
          "Destroy" -> Function[Null, EventRemove[ev]; If[task["TaskStatus"] === "Running", TaskRemove[task]]; ClearAll[task];],
          "Left" -> Function[Null, cnt = 0],
          "Slide" -> Function[Null, task = SetInterval[If[cnt < max, cnt += 1, TaskRemove[task]], 15]]
      }];

      <div class="text-center text-gray-600 m-4 p-4 rounded bg-gray-100 flex flex-col">
          <HTMLCounter/>
          <span class="text-md"><Text/></span>
          <SlideEventListener Id={ev}/>
      </div>
] ]

Options[Stat] = {"Count" -> 1};

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Each `Stat` component is generated with its own scope and counts up independently. You can place them on any slide as many as you want:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Each `Stat` component is generated with its own scope and counts up independently. You can place them on any slide as many as you want:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.slide
# Stats

<Row ImageSize={{Full, Automatic}} Alignment={Center}>
      <Stat Count={11}>Citations</Stat>
      <Stat Count={110}>Hours</Stat>
      <Stat Count={1010}>Symbols</Stat>
</Row>

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
Each counter is self-contained and responds only when the slide is active.

## Widgets with plots
Let's create another example: a custom widget that reacts to slide fragments. For instance, a reactive chart with a flying disk that moves when a fragment appears. Here we use the `Offload` method to switch between datasets provided in options:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Display: markdown
Type: Output
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Each counter is self-contained and responds only when the slide is active.

## Widgets with plots
Let's create another example: a custom widget that reacts to slide fragments. For instance, a reactive chart with a flying disk that moves when a fragment appears. Here we use the `Offload` method to switch between datasets provided in options:

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Fade: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.wlx
PlotWidget[OptionsPattern[]] := Module[{
  data = OptionValue["DataA"],
  disk = OptionValue["DataA"] // Last
},

  With[{
    Canvas = Graphics[{
      ColorData[97][1], Line[data // Offload],
      ColorData[97][3], Disk[disk // Offload, {0.4,0.05}]
    }, Axes -> True, ImageSize -> 500, PlotRange -> {{-0.2, 1.1 5 Pi}, 1.1 {-1, 1}},
       TransitionDuration -> 500],
    uid = CreateUUID[],
    dataA = OptionValue["DataA"],
    dataB = OptionValue["DataB"]
  },
    EventHandler[uid, {
      "fragment-1" -> Function[Null,
        data = dataB;
        disk = dataB // Last;
      ],
      ("Left" | "Destroy" | "Slide") -> Function[Null,
        data = dataA;
        disk = dataB // First;
      ]
      }];

    <div class="flex flex-col gap-y-2">
      <Canvas />
      <div class="fragment invisible">Invisible text</div>
      <SlideEventListener Id={uid} />
    </div>
  ]
]

Options[PlotWidget] = {"DataA" -> {}, "DataB" -> {}};

%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
Props:
  Hidden: true
Type: Input
%-------------------------------------------------------------------------%
.md
